<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meléndez–Quiñónez dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1913">1913</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Meléndez–Quiñónez dynasty was the family group that held the presidency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> from 1913 to 1927: the brothers Carlos and Jorge Meléndez and their brother-in-law Alfonso Quiñónez Molina. Ruling through the official National <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> and controlled elections, they represented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> elite. Their era ended when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pio-romero-bosque">Pío Romero Bosque</a> allowed free elections.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-enrique-araujo">Manuel Enrique Araujo</a> died on 9 February 1913 of wounds from an assassination attempt five days earlier. As the vice president had resigned, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-melendez">Carlos Meléndez</a>, president of the Legislative Assembly and first presidential designate, became provisional president and had the assassins executed by firing squad. Active in politics since the Revolution of the 44 in 1894, he had lost several earlier contests. To run in 1915 he resigned on 29 August 1914, since the constitution barred anyone who had held the presidency in the six months before an election, and handed office to his brother-in-law Alfonso Quiñónez Molina. He won and took office on 1 March 1915.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1918 Jorge Meléndez and Quiñónez founded the National Democratic Party, outside which political activity was soon outlawed; its paramilitary wing, the Red League, intimidated opponents. Rival supporters clashed at the December 1918 municipal elections, killing around 100 people. Carlos resigned through illness on 21 December 1918 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> on 8 October 1919, while Jorge won the 1919 election with 97 percent of the vote. Under Jorge, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arturo-araujo">Arturo Araujo</a> invaded from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> in 1920, and cadets and soldiers attempted coups in February and May 1922. On 25 December 1922 security forces and the Red League fired on a rally for Miguel Tomás Molina, killing around a dozen people, and Quiñónez won the 1923 election unanimously.</p><p>In 1927 Quiñónez chose his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pio-romero-bosque">Pío Romero Bosque</a> as successor, expecting to rule through him. Romero instead removed Quiñónez's appointees, lifted press censorship and allowed demonstrations, and Quiñónez left for exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in June. A coup organised by Jorge failed on 6 December 1927; its two leaders were executed and Jorge fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The family ruled for 14 years and 20 days, from 9 February 1913 to 1 March 1927. They were pragmatic liberals, backed by wealthy landowners and by indigenous Salvadorans promised the return of land. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">Coffee</a> production doubled from its level before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and by 1926 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> was the leading coffee producer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, while corruption and extortion were common. Urban workers formed unions and held the first recorded strike in the country's history in 1920. Carlos's Meléndez Doctrine declared the Gulf of Fonseca a condominium of El Salvador, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Meléndez–Quiñónez dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The constitution of 1886, in force until 1939, gave the liberal coffee republic its framework, with presidents normally chosen by the outgoing government; from 1913 to 1927 members of the <b>Meléndez and Quiñónez families</b> held the presidency in succession.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
